  "query_preview": "..\nFriday through Tuesday: A closed low over SE Oregon will slowly\nmove east today, tracking into southern Idaho. Southeast flow will\ncontinue to draw mid level moisture into SE Washington into the\nCentral Panhandle Mountains. This will keep a chance for showers\ngoing for the Blue Mountains, Palouse, Camas Prairie, and Central\nPanhandle Mountains while the remainder of the region experiences\nwarm and dry conditions. Surface based CAPE of 300-800 J/KG could\nallow allow a few thunderstorms to develop over the high terrain\nin the afternoon. Brief downpours, small hail, lightning, and \nwind gusts up to 30 MPH will be the main hazards with any storms\nthat develop.  Over the weekend the low continues its trek \neastward, reaching Wyoming on Sunday. Upper ridging on the back \nside of this low will build across the Inland NW promoting warm \nand dry conditions with highs mainly in the mid 70s to low 80s. \nThe ridge begins to slowly weaken heading into Monday and Tuesday \nbut mostly clear skies and continued southwest flow will allow \nMonday to be just as warm before slight cooling Tuesday.\n\nWednesday and Thursday: Model clusters have 65 percent of the\nmembers showing an upper ridge holding strong enough for continued\nabove normal temperatures and dry, while 35 percent show more\nsignificant flattening which would mean a continued cooling trend\nand a chance for mainly mountain showers. The forecast is a\ncompromise at this point with highs mainly in the 70s, and a 20\npercent chance of mainly mountain showers. JW",
